El Dorado:
{{Short description|Legendary city in South America}}
{{About|the lost city of gold}}
{{good article}}
[[File:Laguna_de_Guatavita_centro_de_la_leyenda_del_Dorado.jpg|thumb|[[Lake Guatavita]] in the [[Altiplano Cundiboyacense|Cundiboyacan Plateau]], which inspired the legend of El Dorado according to the [[Muisca raft|Muisca Golden ceremony]]|300px]]
'''El Dorado''' ({{IPA|es|el doˈɾaðo|lang}}) is a mythical city of gold supposedly located somewhere in South America. The legend originated in 16th-century Spanish accounts of a ruler known as ''El Dorado'', or the Golden One, who was said to cover himself in gold dust before washing it off in a sacred lake. Over time, the name came to refer not only to the ruler, but also to a lost city or kingdom of immense wealth.

The legend is associated with the [[Muisca]], an indigenous people who inhabited the [[Altiplano Cundiboyacense]] in present-day [[Colombia]]. Later accounts linked the story to the installation ceremony of a new ''[[zipa]]'', involving offerings of gold and other valuables at [[Lake Guatavita]]. The Muisca were skilled goldsmiths, and Spanish expeditions under [[Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada]] reached and conquered their territory in 1537. Later attempts were made to drain Lake Guatavita in search of treasure, and objects associated with the El Dorado tradition, including the [[Muisca raft]], are now held by the [[Gold Museum, Bogotá|Museo del Oro]] in [[Bogotá]].

In the decades that followed, European explorers searched for El Dorado in various parts of South America. Expeditions associated with [[Gonzalo Pizarro]], [[Francisco Orellana]], [[Philipp von Hutten]], [[Pedro de Ursúa]], [[Antonio de Berrio]], and [[Walter Raleigh]] shifted the supposed location of the golden city from the Colombian highlands to the Amazon and the [[Guianas]]. The legend later became associated with [[Lake Parime]], a supposed inland lake that appeared on maps of the region during the 17th century before its existence was rejected by later explorers and geographers.

Although belief in El Dorado as a real place declined, the legend has had a lasting cultural impact. It became a symbol of fabulous wealth, utopia, and obsessive exploration, appearing in works such as [[Voltaire]]'s ''[[Candide]]'', Edgar Allan Poe's "[[Eldorado (poem)|Eldorado]]", and later films, games, and adventure fiction.

== Background ==

[[Christopher Columbus]], the first European known to have reached the Americas after the Scandinavians, made landfall in the Caribbean Islands in 1492. Seeing golden ornaments worn by some of the indigenous inhabitants, he assumed that he had found a prosperous land{{sfn|Sauer|1966|page=34}} and spent several months travelling from island to island in search of the source of the gold.{{sfn|Sauer|1966|pages=23–29}} Although he found no mines, he remained convinced that the newly encountered lands contained great wealth. He promised the [[Catholic monarchs]] of Spain, who sponsored the expedition, that with sufficient assistance, he could "give them as much gold as they have need of".{{sfn|Sauer|1966|page=34}}<ref>{{cite web |url=https://human.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Literature_and_Literacy/American_Literature/Becoming_America_-_An_Exploration_of_American_Literature_from_Precolonial_to_Post-Revolution/01:_Pre-_and_Early_Colonial_Literature/1.03:_Christopher_Columbus_(14511506)/1.3.01:_Letter_of_Discovery_(1493) |title=Letter of Discovery (1493) |date=17 December 2019 |publisher=LibreTexts Humanities |access-date=13 July 2026}}</ref>

European accounts of the newly encountered lands were influenced by older legends of wealthy utopias across the Atlantic.{{sfn|Sánchez|1994|page=202}} The ancient Greeks believed that somewhere in the Atlantic lay the [[Isles of the Blessed]], an earthly paradise with a permanently temperate climate. According to second-century author [[Lucian]], the inhabitants of these islands lived in cities made of gold, ivory, and emeralds.{{sfn|Sánchez|1994|pages=193–194}} The Atlantic also gave its name to the mythical continent of [[Atlantis]], which was said to be home to an advanced civilization rich in gold, silver, and [[orichalcum]].{{sfn|Sánchez|1994|pages=194–195}} During the Middle Ages, stories circulated about the [[Isle of Seven Cities]], a supposed Christian haven that frequently appeared on 15th-century maps and may have influenced the later legend of the [[Seven Cities of Gold]].{{sfn|Sánchez|1994|pages=201–202, 224}} Columbus was also interested in locating the biblical regions of [[Ophir]] and [[Tarshish]], from which [[King Solomon]] was said to have imported vast quantities of treasure.{{sfn|Sánchez|1994|pages=196–197}} He believed that these places, along with the [[Garden of Eden]], were somewhere on the newly discovered continent, and many who followed him shared these beliefs.{{sfn|Sánchez|1994|pages=196–197, 206–207}}

The early Spanish settlers in the Caribbean Islands, however, found their expectations disappointed. The indigenous inhabitants possessed small amounts of gold, but did not mine it systematically,{{sfn|Sauer|1966|pages=61–62}} and the Spanish mining activity quickly exhausted the local supply.{{sfn|Sauer|1966|pages=197–198}} The settlers' attention then turned towards the mainland, and colonies began to be established along the South American coast.{{sfn|Lockhart|Schwartz|1983|page=64}} Despite this unpromising beginning, the [[Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire|conquest of the Aztecs]] by [[Hernán Cortés]] and the [[Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire|conquest of the Incas]] by [[Francisco Pizarro]] soon rekindled European hopes that vast gold deposits remained to be found.{{sfn|Sánchez|1994|pages=220–221}}

