Strait of Ani\u00e1n:
{{Short description|Semi-mythical strait between North America and Asia}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=October 2021}}
[[File:CEM-19-Asiae-nova-description-1610-Jodocus-Hondius-2538.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|A 1610 map showing the Strait of Anián top right, at the approximate location of the [[Bering Strait]]]]
[[File:Straße von Anian.png|thumb|upright=1.3|1687 map showing [[Island of California|Baja California as an island]], with a possible Strait of Anián top left extending east toward [[Hudson Bay]]]]
[[File:Oceano pacifico e Estreito de Anian.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|right|Atlas of [[João Teixeira Albernaz I]], 1643, showing the North Pacific Ocean and the area reached by the navigator [[João da Gama]], including ''islands João da Gama found'' (possibly the [[Kuril Islands]]) or lands further northeast. The mythical or "recognized" Strait of Anián, separating Asia and the Americas, is also shown at top. Part of the known North American coast is possibly widely shifted to the northwest.]]
The '''Strait of Anián''' was a semi-mythical [[strait]], documented from around 1560, that was believed by early modern cartographers to mark the boundary between [[North America]] and [[Asia]] and to permit access to a [[Northwest Passage]] from the [[Arctic Ocean]] to the [[Pacific Ocean|Pacific]]. The true strait was discovered in 1728 and became known as the [[Bering Strait]]. The Strait of Anián had been generally placed nearby, but sometimes appeared as far south as [[geography of California|California]].<ref>{{cite book |first=Samuel Eliot |last=Morison |authorlink=Samuel Eliot Morison |title=The European Discovery of America |publisher=Oxford University Press |location=New York |isbn=0-19-501377-8 |year=1971 }}</ref>{{sfn|Hayes|2001|p=}}

== History ==
[[File:Brobdingnag_map.jpg|thumb|upright=1.2|right|Map of [[Brobdingnag]]; the Strait of Anián can be seen at centre right.]]The source of this idea is unknown. The [[Martin Waldseemüller]] map of 1506 or 1507 shows America and Asia separated. A 1562 map by Paolo Forlani shows Asia and North America joined north of about the latitude of San Diego. The strait probably took its name from Ania, a Chinese province mentioned in a 1559 edition of [[Marco Polo]]'s book. The name probably first appeared on a map issued by the Italian [[Cartography|cartographer]] [[Giacomo Gastaldi]] in 1562.<ref>{{harvnb|Hayes|2001|p=24}}. He says this is a revision of a 1546 map.</ref> It appeared on maps by [[Abraham Ortelius]] (1564), Bolognini Zaltieri (1567) and [[Gerardus Mercator]] (1567). The Zaltieri and Gastaldi maps show it narrow and crooked. Gastaldi and Ortelius have an "Ania" or "Anian" on the east side of the strait. A speculative map of 1578 shows [[Frobisher Strait]] extending all the way across Canada and ending at the Strait of Anián.<ref>{{cite book |first=Glyn |last=Williams |title=Arctic Labyrinth |publisher=University of California Press |location=Berkeley |year=2010 |page=30 |isbn=978-0-520-26627-8 }}</ref> [[Juan de Fuca]], a Greek navigator, sailed in a Spanish expedition in 1592 to seek the fabled Strait of Anián.{{sfn|Brooke-Hitching|2016|pp=14–15}} A 1719 map by [[Herman Moll]] shows the strait as a probable bay at 50° North, north of the [[Island of California]]. The 1726 edition of a map by [[Johannes van Keulen]] shows the strait north of the Island of California with the note that "They say that one can come through this strait to Hudson Bay, but this is not proven."{{sfn|Brooke-Hitching|2016|p=17}}

== Cultural legacy ==
The Strait of Anián can be seen in the map of [[Brobdingnag]] featured in Jonathan Swift's classic 1726 novel ''[[Gulliver's Travels]]''.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Regard |first1=Frédéric |title=The Quest for the Northwest Passage: Knowledge, Nation and Empire, 1576–1806 |date=6 October 2015 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-317-32155-2 |page=39 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2ZVECgAAQBAJ |language=en}}</ref> Anián is also mentioned (as 'Anyan') in the [[John Donne]] poem ''Hymn to God, My God, in My Sickness'' among other places such as the [[Pacific Sea]], [[Jerusalem]], and [[Gibraltar]].

The Strait of Anián is mentioned in the prologue of [[James Clavell]]'s 1975 novel ''[[Shōgun (novel)|Shōgun]]'' as a possible destination for a sailing voyage undertaken by [[Francis Drake]].

