Beatosu and Goblu:
{{Short description|Fictional towns}}
{{Use American English|date=December 2022}}
{{Use mdy dates|date=December 2022}}
{{Infobox fictional location
| name       = Beatosu and Goblu
| image      = goblu beatosu.jpg
| imagesize  = 250px
| caption    = Beatosu (left arrow) and Goblu (right arrow)
| series     = 
| creator    = Peter Fletcher
| type      = [[City]]
| locations  = [[Ohio]]
}}
'''Beatosu''' and '''Goblu''' are two non-existent towns in [[Fulton County, Ohio|Fulton]] and [[Lucas County, Ohio|Lucas]] counties, respectively, in the US state of [[Ohio]]. They were inserted into the 1978–1979 edition of the official state of [[Michigan]] map. The names refer to the slogan of [[University of Michigan]] fans ("[[Let's Go Blue|Go Blue!]]") and a reference to their archrivals from [[Ohio State University]] ("Beat OSU").

== History ==
Peter Fletcher, a Michigan alumnus and chairman of the [[Michigan State Highway Commission|State Highway Commission]] with a "sly sense of humor",<ref name="obituary">{{cite news |last = Gustafson |first = Sven |date = October 1, 2012 |title = Peter B. Fletcher, Longtime Ypsilanti Republican Activist and Philanthropist, Dies at 80 |work = [[AnnArbor.com]] |url = http://www.annarbor.com/news/ypsilanti/longtime-ypsilanti-republican-activist-philanthropist-peter-b-fletcher/ |access-date = October 13, 2016 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180314174209/https://www.annarbor.com/news/ypsilanti/longtime-ypsilanti-republican-activist-philanthropist-peter-b-fletcher/ |archive-date = Mar 14, 2018 }}</ref> ordered the inclusion of the fake towns of "Goblu" (near the real town of [[Bono, Ohio]], off [[Ohio State Route 2|State Route&nbsp;2]]) and "Beatosu" (near [[Archbold, Ohio]], just south of [[Interstate 80 in Ohio|Interstate 80]]/[[Interstate 90 in Ohio|Interstate 90]]/[[Ohio Turnpike]] at exit&nbsp;25).<ref name= barnett>{{cite magazine |last = Barnett |first = Le Roy |url = http://www.michiganhistorymag.com/extra/transportation/maps.pdf |title = Paper Trails: The Michigan Highway Map |magazine = [[Michigan History magazine|Michigan History]] |date = November–December 1999 |volume = 83 |issue = 6 |pages = 18–23 |accessdate = November 25, 2006 |archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20070927051615/http://www.michiganhistorymag.com/extra/transportation/maps.pdf |archivedate = September 27, 2007 }}</ref> On the printed map, the fictitious town names appear in all lowercase letters ("goblu" and "beatosu"), while every other town name is capitalized.  As well, while all real locations are clearly marked, there is no specific dot, boundary line or other marker to identify the precise location of the two fictitious towns.

In a 2008 interview, Fletcher explained that a fellow Michigan alumnus had been teasing him about the [[Mackinac Bridge]] colors: green and white, the colors of [[Michigan State University]]. Fletcher noted that the bridge colors were in compliance with federal highway regulations, so he had no choice in that matter; he did, however, have more control over the state highway map. Fletcher said that he thus ordered a cartographer to insert the two fictitious towns.<ref>{{cite web |last = Garrett |first = Bob |date = October 31, 2008 |title = Beatosu and Goblu, Ohio |url = http://www.michigan.gov/dnr/0,1607,7-153-54463_19313_20652_19271_19357-202571--,00.html |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100311071544/http://www.michigan.gov/dnr/0,1607,7-153-54463_19313_20652_19271_19357-202571--,00.html |archive-date = Mar 11, 2010 |accessdate = August 9, 2009 |work = Archives Image of the Month |publisher = Archives of Michigan, Michigan Historical Center, [[Michigan Department of Natural Resources]] }}</ref>

In their 2012 obituary of Fletcher, ''[[AnnArbor.com]]'' noted that the maps containing the fictional cities had a limited print run and have become collector's items.<ref name=obituary/> The map's publication has also been noted as a good luck charm for the University of [[Michigan Wolverines]] that year; the team beat their arch rivals 14–3 in the 1978 edition of [[Michigan–Ohio State football rivalry|The Game]],<ref name=barnett/> which was once called the greatest North American sports rivalry by a writer for [[ESPN.com]].<ref>{{cite news |title = The 10 Greatest Rivalries |department = The End of the Century |author = ESPN.com |author-link = ESPN.com |work = ESPN.com |date = January 3, 2000 |url = https://www.espn.com/endofcentury/s/other/bestrivalries.html |access-date = December 21, 2006 |archive-date = October 25, 2008 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20081025044126/http://espn.go.com/endofcentury/s/other/bestrivalries.html |url-status = live }}</ref> The map and the rivalry were included in ''Britannica''{{'s}} "One Good Fact" entry for March 11, 2025.<ref>{{cite web |title = One Good Fact |date = March 11, 2025 |url = https://www.britannica.com/one-good-fact/why-did-a-michigan-cartographer-once-make-up-two-cities |work = Britannica |access-date = October 2, 2025 }}</ref>

== In popular culture ==
The fictional ''[[G.I. Joe]]'' comic book character Road Pig is recorded as having been born in Goblu, Michigan.<ref>{{cite web |url = http://www.yojoe.com/filecard/04/roadpig3.shtml |title = Dreadnok Road Pig (v3) G.I. Joe Action Figure |work = YoJoe Archive |access-date = February 24, 2015 |archive-date = June 10, 2012 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20120610233047/http://www.yojoe.com/filecard/04/roadpig3.shtml |url-status = live }}</ref>

The movie ''[[Paper Towns (film)|Paper Towns]]'' refers to the concept of [[Phantom settlement|paper towns]]. In their description, it refers to fake towns on a map created by [[Cartography|cartographers]] to deter copyright infringement. A map extent of Beatosu is shown as an example.{{citation needed|date=December 2016}}

