Llandegley International Airport:
{{Short description|Welsh hoax airport signage}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=February 2022 }}
{{Use British English|date=February 2022}}
[[File:Modern day folly (geograph 3821563) (cropped).jpg|thumb|Sign, next to the [[A44 road|A44]], seen in January 2014]]

'''Llandegley International Airport''' is a spoof located in the village of [[Llandegley]] ({{langx|cy|Llandeglau}}), near [[Llandrindod Wells]], in mid-Wales. No such airport exists, but a sign, erected as a practical joke, announces a forthcoming turn off to it.<ref name="Brockway">{{cite web |last1=Brockway |first1=Derek |title=Llandegley International Airport |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/webarchive/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bbc.co.uk%2Fblogs%2Fwales%2Fentries%2F9291ebe7-50e7-3820-bde6-11db3341ce20 |publisher=[[BBC]] |access-date=20 February 2022 |date=6 May 2010 |type=blog}}</ref><ref name="Mercury=1">{{cite news |title=Midland holidaymakers baffled by international airport that simply doesn't exist |url=https://www.birminghammail.co.uk/news/local-news/midland-holidaymakers-baffled-by-international-airport-231204 |access-date=20 February 2022 |work=[[Sunday Mercury]] |date=5 August 2012 }}</ref><ref name="Mercury-2">{{cite news |title=Planes DID land at spoof Llandegley International Airport |url=https://www.birminghammail.co.uk/news/local-news/planes-did-land-at-spoof-llandegley-international-231235 |access-date=20 February 2022 |work=Sunday Mercury |date=12 August 2012 }}</ref>

[[File:Llandegley International Airport sign (geograph 3603049).jpg|thumb|Sign and [[A44 road|A44]], seen in August 2013]]

Despite Llandegley having no airfield—much less one of international standard—a visitor to Llandegley, Nicholas Whitehead, erected his own road sign, mimicking the UK's [[Worboys Committee|standard Worboys signs]], on private land on the north side of the [[A44 road|A44]] east of [[Crossgates, Powys|Crossgates]] in 2002, showing "Llandegley International [Airport] / {{nowrap|Terminals 1 & 3}} / 2½&nbsp;{{abbr|m|miles}}".<ref name="Brockway" /><ref name="Mercury=1" /> He spent £1,000 on renting the site.<ref name="Mercury=1" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Notes & Queries: Sexual marathons; A mountain village with its own airport?; Horse play; The death of boys' own adventures |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=30 July 2003}}</ref>

The original sign was removed in November 2009, but replaced in 2010 following public outcry.<ref name="Brockway" /><ref name="Coflein">{{Coflein|num=413641 |desc=Llandegley International Airport Sign, A44, Crossgates |access-date=20 February 2022 }}</ref> It was replaced again in April 2012.<ref name="Coflein" /> The version of the sign visible on [[Google Street View]] imagery, captured in April 2021, has the added wording "For Airport Cafe, follow signs for Terminal 1", and a [[flyposting|fly posted]] "No&nbsp;{{abbr|2|to}} Runway&nbsp;2" notice."<ref>{{cite web |title=Google Maps |url=https://www.google.com/maps/@52.2742401,-3.326811,3a,75y,94.66h,85.46t/data=!3m6!1e1!3m4!1s85s_k7i_xaA7nNnha03i-A!2e0!7i16384!8i8192?hl=en |access-date=20 February 2022 }}</ref>

[[File:Llandegley International Airport sign 20250302 175249.jpg|thumb|Sign occupying the site in 2025.]]

The sign has attracted press and television coverage and was mentioned in the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|United Kingdom Parliament]] in 2003 by [[Roger Williams (British politician)|Roger Williams]], the [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|member]] for [[Brecon and Radnorshire (UK Parliament constituency)|Brecon and Radnorshire]].<ref name="Brockway" /><ref>{{Cite Hansard|house=House of Commons |title=Welsh Affairs |url=https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm200203/cmhansrd/vo030312/debtext/30312-27.htm |date=12 March 2003 |column=370 }}</ref>

The sign is listed in the [[Welsh Government]]'s National Monuments Record database, [[Coflein]].<ref name="Coflein" />

A Twitter account, @llandegley_int, has operated in the name of the airport since 2018, and the airport has a 
Facebook page.

In November 2022, the owner decided to end his commitment to the sign, explaining it had cost him a total of over £25,000 to date. He expressed the hope that the Welsh Government's heritage body, {{Lang|cy|[[Cadw]]|italic=no}}, might be interested in funding it in the future as a cultural landmark.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Charles |first=Sue |date=13 November 2022 |title=Man ends 20-year Llandegley airport sign joke that cost £25k |work=[[BBC News]] |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-wales-63600184 |access-date=13 November 2022}}</ref>

After a crowdfunding campaign, in May 2023 a new sign was erected east of the village, where the landowner will allow it to remain without cost.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-wales-65501398 |title=Powys: Spoof £25,000 airport sign makes a welcome return|first=Sue|last=Charles|work=BBC News |date=7 May 2023}}</ref>

